<!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>🌿 Translator's Summary — Kidushin 3</h3>
  <p>This halacha is one of the most comprehensive in all of Likutay Halachos, weaving together halachic structures, Kabbalistic concepts, and deep moral instruction around a central chain of ideas:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The spiritual mechanics of Kidushin:</strong> The coin given in betrothal is not merely a legal mechanism — it draws down the illumination of <em>tzedakah</em>, which soothes the burning of the heart (the craving for money and licentiousness), draws <em>chesed</em>, then <em>da'as</em>, enabling the tzadik to gather and elevate souls (<em>mayin nukvin</em>), ultimately causing Torah to be "born" through the union of the Holy One and His <em>Shechinah</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>The two great lusts:</strong> The lust of licentiousness is the essential trial; the craving for money is its lethal aftermath. Breaking one breaks the other — and <em>tzedakah</em> is the master key to both.</li>
    <li><strong>Guarding the <em>bris</em>:</strong> The <em>bris</em> is the channel of all divine abundance. When it is damaged, holiness is replaced by klipah; the Shechinah is in poverty; the wicked appear wealthy but are never satisfied. The rectification is tzedakah — which reconnects the channel.</li>
    <li><strong>200 and 100 (Kesuvah amounts):</strong> These correspond to doubled providential vision (200 = two × 100), flowing from "ran and returned" (<em>ratzoh v'shuv</em>), Written Torah + Oral Torah, direct light + returning light.</li>
    <li><strong>Written vs. Oral Torah:</strong> Written = general knowledge = faith; Oral = particular attainment = mystical intimacy with G-d. Both are inseparable. The Kesuvah must be in writing to anchor the Oral (mystical) within the Written (revealed).</li>
    <li><strong>Printing holy books:</strong> The act of printing — gathering many letters at once — is the practical realization of "a wise man takes souls" (<em>loke'ach nefashos chacham</em>). Funding the printing of holy books is the highest form of tzedakah and a force against heresy. The <em>shins</em> of the tefilin, made by a press, symbolize this cosmic function.</li>
    <li><strong>Historical urgency:</strong> The author voices deep alarm at the spread of rationalist-philosophical texts (likely Haskalah influences) and calls for printing exponentially more holy books as the spiritual antidote.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="color:#1a5a1a; font-style:italic; margin-top:10px;">
    The organizing metaphor of the entire halacha: <em>Kidushin → Tzedakah → Breaking desire for money → Chesed → Da'as → Taking souls → Mayin nukvin → Yichud → Birth of Torah → All Israel as Torah.</em>
  </p>
</div>

